{
  "task": "judge_question",
  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7609",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268132/assets/7911a60aa67a036ad875f84881a9e0cc69d7dea1ee59d98c72029319aaf04db8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=f(x).$ What is the domain of $y =  f(2-x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's write the function in a form that is convenient for transformations:\n\n$$\ny = f(2-x) =  f(-(x-2))\n$$\n\nWhen performing graph transformations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nSo, to plot the graph of $y = f(-(x-2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, reflect the graph across the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y = f(-x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268132/assets/20a26666e7ea0dc700d8a333a89258b76f6dee022a487221cbf48e7052e8a143.png)\n\n- Finally, we shift the result horizontally by $2$ units to the right  and obtain the graph of $y = f(-(x-2)) .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268132/assets/28c377265ccd55e57bea45df86fb68ad111237cec1f8b1e21dfd8e1b3426fb07.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(2-x)$ is $[0,5).$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268132/observations/lesson-step-28368-e2d33fe6f439f67199d7.html",
